    reg {
        name = "SATA related clock and soft reset control register";
        desc = "
        Clock gates, reference clock select and the eight soft resets of the AHCI SATA host at 0x10080000 and its PHY (DS 14.7.5, `datasheet.txt` lines 57113-57260). Reset bits are asserted (1) after power-on
        by the hardware default of 0; the bring-up order is: PERI_CRG45 bit 11 = 0 (150 MHz reference), deassert `hrst_sata_s` (bit 1), deassert `rst_sataphy_s` (bit 0), program the PHY registers, pulse
        `rst_sataphy_s`, then deassert `rst_sata_s` (2), `rst_tx0_s` (3), `rst_rx0_s` (5), `rst_alive_s` (7) (and the port 1 resets 4 and 6 on Hi3520D/Hi3515A; Hi3515C has one port).
        Vendor driver (`ahci_sys_hi3520d_defconfig.c`, `HI_SATA_PERI_CRG` = CRG + 0xB4): `hi_sata_clk_open` sets bits 10, 9, 8 (`sata_cken`, `sata0_cken`, `sata1_cken`), `hi_sata_phy_clk_sel` clears bits 11
        and 12, `hi_sata_unreset` / `hi_sata_reset` clear / set bit 1, `hi_sata_phy_(un)reset` bit 0, `hi_sata_clk_(un)reset` set / clear bits 2-7 together. The SATA PHY analog options are in SYS_CTRL
        PERIPHCTRL3 / PERIPHCTRL25. The data sheet text at 14.7.5.1 says 'disable the clock by setting sata0_cken and sata1_cken to 1' (typo: 0).
         Hardware (Hi3520D V100 board, read on hardware 2026-11-08, stock U-Boot prompt after a cold boot): reads 0x00000000, i.e. all SATA clocks OFF (sata*_cken = 0) and no reset asserted, while the data sheet reset is 0x00000700 (clocks on). Open question: the BootROM / boot table may gate the SATA clocks, or the data sheet reset is wrong (PERI_CRG46, the USB equivalent, reads its data-sheet reset 0xFF). The reset is left at the data sheet value until it is known which. Linux must enable the clocks before touching the SATA block (the SATA host registers were not read for that reason).";

